          {/* STRATEGY 2 */}
          <section id="strategy-2-ben-duoc" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-5">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                Strategy 2: Choosing Between Ben Dinh and Ben Duoc (Location, Crowds & Heritage)
              </h2>
            </div>

            <div class="text-slate-700 text-[15.5px] leading-relaxed space-y-4">
              <p>
                First-time visitors often assume Cu Chi is a single site with one entrance. In reality, two separate sectors are open to the public, spaced about 15 kilometres apart: <strong>Ben Dinh</strong> (<em>Bến Đình</em>) and <strong>Ben Duoc</strong> (<em>Bến Dược</em>).
              </p>
              <p>
                There is a widespread myth on travel forums that Ben Dinh is "fake" while Ben Duoc is "the only real one." That is historically inaccurate. Vietnam's 2026 UNESCO submission explicitly identifies both sectors as the surviving strongholds that best preserve the layout, depth, and three-tier vertical architecture of the wartime network, with roughly four kilometres of passages conserved between them.
              </p>
              <p>
                The real difference between them comes down to geography, crowd volume, and atmosphere.
              </p>
              <p>
                Importantly, UNESCO's 2026 documentation notes that both sites preserve a <strong>three-level tunnel architecture</strong> connected by vertical trapdoor hatches. Ben Duoc's main spine tunnel features three distinct vertical levels, with its deepest tiers originally engineered to withstand heavy aerial bombardment. Ben Dinh shares a similar three-level design, though its subterranean passages are generally shallower than those at Ben Duoc.
              </p>

              {/* Comparison Table */}
              <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
                <table class="w-full border-collapse bg-white rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-xs border border-slate-200/80 text-sm">
                  <thead>
                    <tr class="bg-slate-900 text-white font-serif">
