                      <td class="p-4 text-slate-600">More likely to encounter groups</td>
                      <td class="p-4 text-slate-600">Longer journey (~20 km additional drive)</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>

              <p>
                <strong>Why Ben Dinh Draws More Buses:</strong> Ben Dinh is located in Nhuận Đức, closer to central Ho Chi Minh City (~50 km). Because it saves nearly an hour of round-trip driving through suburban traffic, the vast majority of budget and commercial bus tours head here. That makes the parking lots fuller and the briefings louder, particularly between 09:30 AM and midday.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>The Ben Duoc Setting:</strong> Ben Duoc lies roughly 70 km northwest of downtown Saigon in An Nhơn Tây. That extra 20 km of driving acts as an effective natural filter—large 45-seat tour coaches rarely make the longer journey. The site spreads out along the banks of the Saigon River under mature forest canopy, giving the entire visit a calm, unhurried atmosphere.
              </p>
              <p>
                Crucially, Ben Duoc offers far more than an underground crawl. The grounds house the <strong>Ben Duoc Memorial Temple</strong> (a dignified tribute honouring more than 44,000 fallen soldiers and civilians) and the <strong>Recreated Liberation Zone</strong> (<em>Khu tái hiện Vùng Giải phóng</em>), which depicts wartime village life with thatch cottages, rice-paper drying racks, and artisans demonstrating how guerrilla fighters lived, worked, and wore the iconic <a href="/khan-ran-nam-bo" class="text-amber-800 hover:text-amber-900 font-semibold underline underline-offset-2 decoration-amber-300">khăn rằn scarf</a>. If you want a deep dive into wartime history rather than just a quick tunnel walk, Ben Duoc is well worth the extra drive. Both sites feature sections subtly adapted to accommodate visitors safely.
              </p>

