          {/* WHAT IS ACTUALLY ORIGINAL AT CU CHI? */}
          <section id="what-is-original" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-5">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                What Is Actually Original at Cu Chi? (Preserved vs. Untouched)
              </h2>
            </div>

            <div class="text-slate-700 text-[15.5px] leading-relaxed space-y-4">
              <p>
                Travel blogs and guidebooks frequently repeat that the Cu Chi network spans "200 to 250 kilometres." That historical figure reflects the network at its greatest wartime expansion, but it often puzzles first-time visitors who expect an underground labyrinth stretching endlessly in every direction.
              </p>
              <p>
                In the five decades since 1975, unmaintained earthen passages in tropical soil naturally collapsed, flooded during monsoon seasons, or gave way to post-war farming. What visitors explore today are carefully conserved heritage sectors.
              </p>
              <p>
                Vietnam's 2026 UNESCO submission confirms that <strong>approximately four kilometres</strong> of the surviving tunnel network are actively preserved across Ben Duoc and Ben Dinh. These two sectors were specifically conserved because they best preserve the authentic multi-tier vertical architecture: Level 1 (roughly 3 metres deep, sheltering living quarters and weapons workshops), Level 2 (roughly 6 metres deep, used during artillery barrages), and Level 3 (up to 8 to 10 metres deep, carved beneath impervious clay layers to withstand heavy aerial bombardment).
              </p>

              {/* Insight Box */}
              <div class="bg-slate-50 p-5 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200/90 my-4 space-y-2">
                <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
                  <span class="text-amber-600 text-base">💡</span> Preserved vs. Untouched: What "Authenticity" Really Means
                </div>
                <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                  Neither site is frozen in amber. Passageways open to visitors have been subtly shored up with timber and brick, fitted with discreet low-voltage lighting, and in parts widened slightly so international guests can pass through safely. The authenticity lies in the original engineering, the tactical layout, and the real red earth beneath your feet—not in pretending that visitors are crawling through raw, unreinforced dirt. Ben Duoc simply preserves a quieter, more expansive forest environment.
                </p>
              </div>

