          <section id="above-vs-below-ground" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-5">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                Above vs. Below Ground: The Reality of Crawling Through Tunnels
              </h2>
            </div>

            <div class="text-slate-700 text-[15.5px] leading-relaxed space-y-4">
              <p>
                Claustrophobia is the single biggest hesitation travellers have when deciding whether to visit Cu Chi. Many imagine they will be forced to spend hours crawling through dark, suffocating holes underground.
              </p>
              <p>
                The reality is quite the opposite: <strong>roughly 85 to 90 percent of your visit takes place in the open air</strong>, walking shaded trails through secondary forest. Going underground is entirely optional.
              </p>

              <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-6">
                <div class="bg-white p-5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 space-y-2 shadow-xs">
                  <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
                    <span class="text-emerald-600 text-base">✓</span> Above Ground by Default
                  </div>
                  <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs leading-relaxed">
                    Nobody is forced to crawl. The most compelling elements of Cu Chi—camouflaged trapdoors hidden beneath leaves, termite-mound air vents, booby-trap displays, weapons workshops, documentary film screenings, and the Hoang Cam smokeless kitchen—are all viewed above ground on forest footpaths.
                  </p>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-white p-5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 space-y-2 shadow-xs">
                  <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm flex items-center gap-2">
                    <span class="text-blue-600 text-base">🚪</span> Short Crawls with Regular Exits
                  </div>
                  <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs leading-relaxed">
                    If you do choose to head underground, the visitor-accessible tunnels are broken into short, clearly marked segments (usually 20, 40, or 60 metres). Emergency exit hatches are installed every 15 to 20 metres, so you can pop back up into the fresh air whenever you feel ready.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>

              {/* Image: Booby traps and outdoor exhibits */}
              <figure class="my-6">
                <img 
                  src="https://media.thericetour.com/uploads/guide-showing-punji-stick-traps-cu-chi-tunnels-tour.webp" 
                  alt="Local site guide demonstrating concealed punji stick traps and woodland defensive mechanisms at Cu Chi Tunnels" 
                  width="1280"
                  height="720"
                  class="w-full rounded-2xl shadow-md object-cover max-h-[440px]" 
                  loading="lazy" 
                />
                <figcaption class="text-center text-sm text-slate-500 mt-3 font-serif italic">
                  Open-air exploration: More than 85% of a Cu Chi visit takes place above ground, examining ingenious trapdoors and defensive mechanisms along forest trails.
                </figcaption>
              </figure>

              {/* Tunnel Size & Mobility Warning */}
              <div class="bg-amber-50/70 p-5 rounded-2xl border border-amber-200 my-4 space-y-2">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2 font-bold text-amber-900 text-sm sm:text-base">
                  <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-amber-600" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 9v2m0 4h.01m-6.938 4h13.856c1.54 0 2.502-1.667 1.732-3L13.732 4c-.77-1.333-2.694-1.333-3.464 0L3.34 16c-.77 1.333.192 3 1.732 3z"/></svg>
                  Tunnel Dimensions & Physical Demands
                </div>
                <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-amber-950/90 leading-relaxed">
                  The challenge underground is not just claustrophobia; it is physical exertion. The passages are tight—frequently under 80 centimetres wide—and require duck-walking, crouching, or crawling on all fours through heavy, humid air. For travellers over six feet tall (180 cm), larger-framed guests, or anyone with bad knees or mobility limitations, navigating underground is genuinely strenuous. Skipping the crawl takes nothing away from your visit; you can examine every major exhibit, touch the artifacts, and absorb the history without setting foot underground.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
