          {/* CAN YOU VISIT WITHOUT A TOUR? */}
          <section id="visiting-without-a-tour" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-5">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                Can You Visit Cu Chi Tunnels Without a Tour?
              </h2>
            </div>

            <div class="text-slate-700 text-[15.5px] leading-relaxed space-y-4">
              <p>
                <strong>Yes, absolutely.</strong> You do not need an organized tour package to visit either Ben Dinh or Ben Duoc. Many independent travellers hire a private driver or book a car through ride-hailing apps like Grab to make the trip.
              </p>
              <p>
                When you purchase your entry ticket at the gate, your admission automatically includes an <strong>official on-site guide</strong>. These local guides are stationed at the entrance, speak English and Vietnamese, and walk with you through the exhibits, demonstrating trapdoors and explaining how the underground network functioned. Because they live in Cu Chi and work exclusively on-site, their historical knowledge is often deeper and far more personal than a standard bus-tour commentary.
              </p>

              {/* Guide Quality Card */}
              <div class="bg-blue-50/70 p-5 rounded-2xl border border-blue-200 my-4 space-y-2">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2 font-bold text-blue-900 text-sm sm:text-base">
                  <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-blue-600" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg>
                  Why Guide Quality Matters as Much as Crowd Timing
                </div>
                <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-blue-950/90 leading-relaxed">
                  Crowd timing is only half the battle. Cu Chi is a site where context is everything: without someone to explain why a mound of dirt has disguised ventilation holes, or how smoke was filtered through subterranean chambers, the forest trails can feel like an arbitrary outdoor walk. If you travel independently, always make sure to connect with your designated site guide at the entrance.
                </p>
